---
/**
 * The corrections policy, and the dated log it commits to.
 *
 * Eight entries, newest first. The bar for appearing here is that the error changed what
 * this site recommends, or what a reader would do about it. Errors that moved a decimal,
 * broke a naming collision inside the research, or restated a statistic without touching a
 * conclusion are fixed beside the figure they belong to and are not narrated here: a log
 * nobody can finish reading protects nobody.
 *
 * Three entries dated 2026-08-24 are the same class of error and are the reason the page
 * still exists in this form. Each one is a number that travelled away from the comparison
 * that produced it.
 *
 * `#two-portfolios` is the only entry that is still open. Nothing was re-run to close it,
 * because the run that would close it does not exist yet. Do not quietly reword it into
 * the past tense.
 *
 * Every heading id is a permanent anchor. If another page ever links to a single
 * correction by that id, the id is renamed only alongside the links that point at it.
 *
 * Every date is the day the corrected figure went live, which is checkable in the commit
 * history. Do not invent one for an entry whose date cannot be established: leave the
 * entry off instead.
 *
 * A note on units. `placement-restated` is quoted as a percentage a year, the same way its
 * source stated the amount. Every sentence around it says that amount in money instead, and no
 * number on this page is typed into a paragraph without its record.
 */
import Callout from "~/components/Callout.astro";
import Figure from "~/components/Figure.astro";
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Corrections"
  description="What this project published, what turned out to be wrong with it, what caught the error, and what changed. Eight dated entries, including a return figure that was never measured and was deleted rather than replaced with a better guess."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink">Corrections</h1>

    <p class="lead">What this site published, what turned out to be wrong with it, and what changed as a result.
    Every date is the day the corrected figure went live.</p>

    <p>Errors get found here often enough that the log is a working document rather than an apology. Most of them
    are decimals, wording, and arguments inside the research that never reached a page, and those are fixed in
    silence beside the number they belong to. The eight below are the ones that changed what this site recommends
    or what a reader would do about it.</p>

    <p>If you read one, read <a href="#trend-premium">the trend-following return</a>. It was quoted confidently
    here for most of the project&rsquo;s life, nobody had ever measured it, and when we worked out what it actually
    was we deleted it rather than printing a better guess.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2 id="policy">What happens when we find one</h2>

    <p>Four rules, written down before they were needed. A site that fixes its numbers in silence is
    indistinguishable from a site that was right all along, and a reader has no way to tell which one they are
    reading.</p>

    <p>The number is corrected everywhere it appears, and an entry lands here with its date.</p>

    <p>A test frozen before it ran is never edited afterwards, even when the test was the thing that was wrong. Its
    defect is recorded on the page that reads it, because a bar you can revise after seeing the result is worth
    nothing.</p>

    <p>A withdrawn number is deleted rather than replaced with a better guess. A blank looks like an oversight and
    a plausible number looks like work, so this is harder than it sounds.</p>

    <p>Where a correction has a mechanical fix, the fix is code that refuses. One entry below ended with a function
    that now raises an error on the exact mistake that produced it.</p>

    <Callout kind="do-this">
      <p>
        Found one we missed? <a href="/about/">Who writes this</a> says how to tell us. A number, its source, and
        the sentence you think it contradicts is enough, and you do not need to be polite about it.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2 id="log">The log</h2>

    <p>Newest first.</p>

    <h3 id="behaviour-gap-attribution">A number of ours published under somebody else&rsquo;s name</h3>

    <p class="eyebrow" data-numeric>Corrected 2026-08-24</p>

    <p><strong>What we believed.</strong> That Morningstar had measured the gap between what a fund earns and what
    its investors earn at <Figure id="behaviour-gap" inline /> for the kind of portfolio published here, and the
    figure was credited to them.</p>

    <p><strong>What was wrong.</strong> The name on it. Morningstar&rsquo;s published gap over the ten years to
    2025 is about 1.2 points a year: the average dollar invested earned 8.7% a year while the funds that dollar
    sat in earned 9.9%. That describes a population rather than a person, and Morningstar disclaims the individual
    reading, because for every buyer there is a seller. The smaller figure is ours. It is the average of 20,000
    simulated ten-year paths with level monthly contributions, run in this project, and the spread around it is
    2.60 points, which is many times the average itself. The arithmetic was sound and the attribution was
    invented.</p>

    <p><strong>What caught it.</strong> Reading the cited study to see whether it contained the number being
    credited to it. It did not.</p>

    <p><strong>What changed.</strong> The figure now names this project as its source and says how it was
    produced, with its spread beside it. Morningstar&rsquo;s own gap is quoted separately, at its own size, and
    the two are never added together or swapped for one another.</p>

    <h3 id="timing-benchmark">A timing rule&rsquo;s gain measured against one thing and reported against another</h3>

    <p class="eyebrow" data-numeric>Corrected 2026-08-24</p>

    <p><strong>What we believed.</strong> That selling out of stocks when the market falls below its 200-day
    average came out <Figure id="timing-rule-gap" inline showUnit={false} /> percentage points a year ahead of
    holding. The home page said so.</p>

    <p><strong>What was wrong.</strong> The comparison. The rule sits in cash about a quarter of the time, so it
    holds less in shares on average than somebody who never sells. The +0.74 is measured against a blend that
    holds the same average amount in shares, which is the comparison that isolates the rule rather than the share
    exposure. Against simply holding, the same rule was 0.73 points a year behind over the same century of US
    data. Two comparisons, nearly the same number, opposite signs, and the page named the wrong one.</p>

    <p><strong>What caught it.</strong> Writing every performance figure on the site next to what it was measured
    against, and finding one whose sentence and whose own record disagreed.</p>

    <p><strong>What changed.</strong> The number never appears now without the comparison in the same clause, and
    both readings are printed together. The verdict on the rule is unchanged and was never the thing in dispute:
    at +0.74 against a smallest detectable gap of
    <Figure id="timing-rule-floor" inline showUnit={false} /> points a year,
    <a href="/evidence/market-timing/">we can&rsquo;t tell</a>.</p>

    <h3 id="rebalancing-policy">A rebalancing cost belonging to a different portfolio and a different rule</h3>

    <p class="eyebrow" data-numeric>Corrected 2026-08-24</p>

    <p><strong>What we believed.</strong> That rebalancing cost this portfolio 0.387% a year, and the number was
    printed to three decimal places as though it were money leaving a reader&rsquo;s account.</p>

    <p><strong>What was wrong.</strong> Three things inside one figure. It was a monthly rebalancing rule rather
    than the annual review on a 25% band this site recommends. It was measured on a different set of holdings, a
    three-way mix of regional stock indexes. And it was a difference in growth rates before any trading cost, which
    is not the same quantity as a cost.</p>

    <p><strong>What caught it.</strong> Putting the site&rsquo;s own rebalancing policy beside the number being
    quoted for it, and finding they were different rules on different holdings.</p>

    <p><strong>What changed.</strong> The page names the policy and what it was compared against in the same
    clause. On this portfolio&rsquo;s own policy over 427 months, the difference against never trading at all is
    <Figure id="rebalance-return" inline /> and the smallest difference that test could have seen was 1.81
    percentage points, so it reads as nothing in either direction.
    <a href="/evidence/rebalancing/">Rebalance to control how much risk you hold</a>, which is settled, and expect
    nothing else from it.</p>

    <h3 id="two-portfolios">Two different portfolios, both called the recommendation</h3>

    <p class="eyebrow" data-numeric>Corrected 2026-08-24</p>

    <p><strong>What we believed.</strong> That the seven-fund portfolio published here was the seven-fund portfolio
    that had been tested, and that a <span data-numeric>30%</span> trend weight had been measured as better than a
    <span data-numeric>25%</span> one.</p>

    <p><strong>What was wrong.</strong> Two things, and the second is the one that matters. The test we froze
    before running it names a 25% portfolio as the recommendation, because that is what we recommended on the day
    it was frozen. We then moved five points out of a plain US index fund and into the stacked trend fund and
    relabelled nothing, so a reader could find two different seven-fund portfolios each presented as the
    portfolio.</p>

    <p>And the result that moved the weight, <Figure id="thirty-over-twenty-five" inline showUnit={false} />, was
    never a comparison between those two. It scored the seven funds at 25% against an earlier eight-fund draft at
    30%, which differs in four of its holdings as well. The record said "same seven funds". It was not the same
    seven funds, and at the time nobody had run these seven at 30%.</p>

    <p><strong>What caught it.</strong> Reading the frozen file&rsquo;s own list of what it scored, rather than the
    sentence the result was being quoted in.</p>

    <p><strong>What changed.</strong> The 30% portfolio is named as the recommendation everywhere and the 25% one
    as what was tested, and every figure drawn from that run says which of the two it belongs to. The result is
    still published, and now carries what it compared. Experiment 016f has since run the matched pair: the seven
    funds at 30% lead the same seven at 25% by <Figure id="matched-thirty-over-twenty-five" inline showUnit={false} />
    at what trend paid over the window, and <a href="/portfolios/with-trend/">portfolio four</a> quotes that gap
    at the lower return we would guess going forward as well.</p>

    <p>What has not changed is the number, because this one is still open. Every other entry here was closed by
    re-running something; this one was not, because the run that would close it has never been done. Strip the
    trend and the borrowing out of both and the two sets of holdings are worth <span data-numeric>0.80</span> and
    <span data-numeric>0.79</span> percentage points a year against the same control over the same months, which is
    close enough that the trend weight is the plausible remaining explanation. That is an argument rather than a
    paired measurement. The matched pair is written down as an open question and has not been run. You can read
    what it means for the portfolio at <a href="/portfolios/with-trend/">the seven-fund page</a>.</p>

    <h3 id="trend-premium">A trend-following return we had never measured</h3>

    <p class="eyebrow" data-numeric>Corrected 2026-08-22</p>

    <p><strong>What we believed.</strong> That trend following would return
    <Figure id="trend-premium-withdrawn" inline showUnit={false} /> percentage points a year from here. The figure
    sized a position, and was then used to argue against holding that position at all.</p>

    <p><strong>What was wrong.</strong> Nobody had measured it. It was the 2012 to 2025 stretch&rsquo;s own
    compounded average with a 1.50% fee taken off, compared against a break-even computed on a different basis,
    with the same fee charged a second time by the model that consumed it. Three errors inside one number: a
    variance term worth 0.77 points, a fee counted twice, and a single figure used with no range when its own range
    ran from −2.67 to +11.00 and so contained the 10.98 it was being used to overturn.</p>

    <p><strong>What caught it.</strong> Running the arithmetic backwards. Add the fee back, add the variance term
    back, and 1.80 returns 4.07, which is that same file&rsquo;s own average on the basis the break-even used. A
    forecast that reconstructs a past average to the second decimal is not a forecast.</p>

    <p><strong>What changed.</strong> The sign of the recommendation. On the published basis the position
    subtracted 0.35 percentage points a year against the same portfolio without it; corrected, it adds 0.34. The
    recommended weight moved to <span data-numeric>25%</span>.</p>

    <p>The number itself is gone, and nothing replaced it. Defensible estimates run
    <span data-numeric>2.84</span> to <span data-numeric>7.18</span> percentage points a year depending on which
    construction you believe, the break-even is <span data-numeric>2.96</span>, and picking one out of that spread
    would be choosing the answer rather than measuring it.
    <a href="/evidence/trend-following/">What is and is not known about trend following</a> is the standing
    account.</p>

    <h3 id="borrowed-comparison">A borrowed portfolio compared against an index that borrowed nothing</h3>

    <p class="eyebrow" data-numeric>Corrected 2026-08-22</p>

    <p><strong>What we believed.</strong> That the construction beat the index, so the construction was doing
    something.</p>

    <p><strong>What was wrong.</strong> The construction holds $1.32 of assets for every dollar put into it and the
    index holds $1.00. You beat that index in good years and trail it in bad ones whatever else you do. Take the
    credit on the way up and you take the blame on the way down.</p>

    <p><strong>What caught it.</strong> Building a comparison that borrows exactly as much as the portfolio does,
    and running everything again against it.</p>

    <p><strong>What changed.</strong> At matched borrowing the worst fall was
    <Figure id="levered-comparison-drawdown" inline showUnit={false} />. The unflattering consequence is printed
    too. The one whole-portfolio comparison that clears the smallest gap its own test could see, the recommended
    portfolio against the investor&rsquo;s original at −0.50 percentage points a year, is mostly measuring 5.4
    points of borrowing rather than anything about how the portfolio is built.</p>

    <h3 id="cross-benchmark-sum">Adding up savings measured against different comparisons</h3>

    <p class="eyebrow" data-numeric>Corrected 2026-08-22</p>

    <p><strong>What we believed.</strong> That putting each fund in the account that taxes it least was worth about
    $38 to $55 a year on every $10,000. It was the largest new number in the recommendation.</p>

    <p><strong>What was wrong.</strong> Four things at once. The range had been truncated, dropping a reading worth
    $18 on that same $10,000. A rebalancing cost avoided had been booked as a saving worth $14, on the same page
    that says a cost avoided is not a saving. Choosing which shares to sell and never selling at all had both been
    booked, and each excludes the other. And 94.7% of the top figure came from one holding&rsquo;s undistributed
    foreign income.</p>

    <p>Underneath all four sat one structural error. Some lines were measured against a cheap index fund and some
    against the portfolio you would otherwise own. A cheap index, your own alternative and the average investor are
    three different questions, and their answers never add.</p>

    <p><strong>What caught it.</strong> An independent re-derivation, which reproduced every priority, the credit
    cap and all twelve cells exactly. The calculation was right. What was booked from it was not.</p>

    <p><strong>What changed.</strong> The line now reads <Figure id="placement-restated" inline showUnit={false} />
    against a control an investor could hold, which is about $2 to $7 a year on every $10,000, with the conditional
    range beside it and added to nothing. The function that adds these lines together now raises an error on a sum
    across different comparisons. <a href="/tools/placement/">The account-placement calculator</a> uses the
    restated line.</p>

    <h3 id="qualified-dividends">Assuming every dividend was the good kind</h3>

    <p class="eyebrow" data-numeric>Corrected 2026-08-22</p>

    <p><strong>What we believed.</strong> That fund dividends qualify for the long-term tax rate. It is the
    standard simplifying assumption and nobody had checked it.</p>

    <p><strong>What was wrong.</strong> It had reversed the account-placement ranking for emerging-market stocks,
    which is exactly the decision the section existed to make.</p>

    <p><strong>What caught it.</strong> Reading the sponsors&rsquo; own filed fractions, which took an afternoon.
    VEA files 66.27%. VWO files <Figure id="qualified-fraction-vwo" inline showUnit={false} />. IEMG 34.82%, AVES
    44.48%, IDMO 25%, DFIV 100%.</p>

    <p><strong>What changed.</strong> Every placement conclusion that predated the filed fractions had to be worked
    again rather than restated, which is slow and unpleasant and the only honest option.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2 id="pattern">What these have in common</h2>

    <p>Six of the eight were caught by the project arguing with itself: a hostile review, a re-derivation of
    arithmetic somebody had already done once, a comparison built to have a known answer, a frozen file read beside
    the sentence quoting it. Two came from checking a number against the source it named, and finding the source
    said something else.</p>

    <p>Most of them were arithmetic that was correct when somebody did it. What broke came afterwards, when the
    number left the page that made it true and travelled without its window, its comparison or its range. That is
    the failure this site is built against, and it is why every number here drags its source behind it.</p>

    <p>One of the eight is still open, and none of this means the current numbers are right.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
